    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d70">

                <pb facs="0226" n="108" sameAs="frus:frus1928v02/pg108"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">158.429/189: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Montreal</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Halstead</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Montreal</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-03-25"><hi rendition="#italic">March 25,
                        1927</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received 2:30 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Referring to my despatches No. 2968, and 2986.<anchor n="76" xml:id="d70-f1"/>
                    Typhoid epidemic continues. Cream and milk shipments from western Quebec <pb
                        facs="0227" n="109" xml:id="pg109"/> might spread infection to the United
                    States. Shall I refuse invoices? Health officer in Montreal states no control of
                    cream and milk exported. Have informed Collector of Customs, Rouses Point
                    regarding one shipment cream today …</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Albert Halstead</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
